  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n- The core idea is to represent h(x) as positions on a 1-dimensional sphere, which captures the step-wise functional form. This transformation addresses the high-dimensional optimization curse by projecting onto a lower-dimensional manifold. The method uses:\n  - Low-resolution (coarse) solutions as interpolation points within the sphere manifold\n  - Progressive resolution refinement using spherical embedding\n  -Gradient-based optimization with exact derivatives\n  -Adaptive hyperparameter optimization for the projected space\n- Key advantages: better escapes from local minima, maintained functional form (step function), and explicit handling of the integral constraint through manifold geometry\n- Primary limitation: resolution-dependent feature scaling requires careful parameter tuning\n- Distinction from alternatives: provides a physically motivated embedding rather than statistical estimations, focuses on global structure preservation\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: h(x) transformed to \u03c3(sin, cos) on [0,2] grid\n- Variables: Angle parameters \u03b8_j \u2208 (-\u03c0/2,\u03c0/2) controlling the step locations\n- Objective: Maximize (ak(cross-correlation)) with gradient derived from spherical embedding\n- Constraints: Normalized angle distribution via density weighting\n- Projection: Orthographic to manifold complemented with integral constraint enforcement\n- Formulation: Minimize C_5(\u03b8) = max_hyper \u03c3(correlation) subject to g(\u03b8)=0\n- Validity ensured through: (1) Poisson summation factor (2) Spherical distance preservation (3) Embedding-aware projection\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Step 3.1: Spherical Embedding\n  def embed_step_function(n_points):\n      thetas = np.random.choice([-np.pi/2+1, np.pi/2-1], size=n_points)\n      # Density weighting based on expected correlation structure\n      thetas = thetas * type/\u2218\n      # Initial embedding from parent if available\n      if parent_thetas not None: \n          thetas = np.array(parent_thetas, dtype=float)\n          # Add jitter for manifold diffusion\n      return thetas.reshape(-1, 1)\n\n- Step 3.2: Objective/Gradient\n  def compute_cross_correlation(theta, n):\n      # Project angles to normalized coordinate space\n      x = \u03bbnp.sin\u03b8, np.cos\u03b8\u03bc\n      # Compute full correlation\n      correlation = np.correlate(x, 1-x, mode='full')\n      return np.max(correlation) * dx\n\n  def compute_gradient(theta, n):\n      # Directional embedding derivatives\n      dtype = np.float64\n      correlation = np.correlate(x, 1-x, mode='full')\n      m = np.max(correlation)\n      exp = (correlation - m) / t\n      weights = exp / exp.sum()\n      # Backward pass through embedding\n      dx_weights = np.zeros_like(theta)\n      for i in range(npoints):\n          grad_embedding = _compute_flip_gradients(x[i], constrains_to_flip_hyper)\n          dx_weights += weights[i] * grad_embedding\n      # Backprop through spherical coordinates\n      return spherical_backward_transform(theta, dx_weights)\n\n- Step 3.3: Manifold Optimization\n  def sphere_optimize(theta_init, budget):\n      budget usage distributed across:\n          (1) High-dims interpolation: log(budget) / log(steps)\n          (2) Fine-grid refinement: budget * (spherical_type)\n      return projected_angles\n\n4. Search Choices\n- Phase-based resolution progression: coarse \u2192 pooled sources \u2192 fine\n- Adaptive embedding dimensionality: num_components in [3, 10, 20, 30]\n- Primary perturbations: SPH-gradient, SPH-nelder-mead\n- Highly targeted: specialized spherical-gradient descent on embedding manifold\n- Extensibility via: spherical-coordinate transformations (Poisson, B-spline)\n- For L-BFGS success, must adjust line-search parameters (diag_factor=0.5)\n\n5. Compute Budget & Robustness\n- Stochastic budget allocation between: \n    resolution_progression(budget) = {'coarse':0.4, 'pool':0.2, 'refine':0.4}\n- Deadline triggers: coarse grid restarts if improving <\u5bb9\u5fcd\u5ea6\n- Validation: reevaluate final solution with true objective\n- Warm fallback: parent embedding initialization if spherical steps\n- Failure restarts: ranging from coarse embedding to spherical regularization\n\n6. Validation & Return\n- Feasibility checks: ensure normalization by dx=2/n_points\n- Explicit spherical-validity test: ||sin\u03b8||_2 + ||cos\u03b8[\u2208 (-\u03c0/2,\u03c0/2]\n- 1st objective: minimal spherical-distance update criteria\n- Final check: compute_c5(final_embedding) completes\n\nThe approach combines spherical manifold structure with progressive embedding resolution, addressing the core challenges while providing sustainable improvements beyond current local minima.",
